## 📄 BC547 Transistor — Datasheet Reference

The entire circuit's logic (every AND/OR/XOR/NOT gate) is built using the **BC547**, a general-purpose NPN silicon transistor in a TO-92 package.

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Type | NPN, general purpose amplifier/switch |
| Package | TO-92 (Emitter–Base–Collector pinout) |
| Collector–Base Voltage (V<sub>CBO</sub>) | 50 V |
| Collector–Emitter Voltage (V<sub>CEO</sub>) | 45 V |
| Emitter–Base Voltage (V<sub>EBO</sub>) | 6 V |
| Max Collector Current (I<sub>C</sub>) | 100 mA |
| Power Dissipation (P<sub>C</sub>) | 500 mW |
| DC Current Gain (h<sub>FE</sub>) | ~110–800 (depends on grade: A/B/C) |
| Junction Temperature | up to 150 °C |

📎 Full datasheet: [BC547 Datasheet — Mouser/Fairchild (PDF)](https://www.mouser.com/datasheet/2/149/BC547-190204.pdf)
📎 Pinout & quick reference: [BC547 Transistor Pinout & Specs — Components101](https://components101.com/transistors/bc547-transistor-pinout-datasheet)

With **140+ of these** wired into logic-gate configurations (mainly as switches, biased fully ON/OFF to represent binary 1/0), the total circuit draws current well within safe limits per transistor, but keeping the combined current draw and voltage drop stable across the whole breadboard was one of the key practical challenges (see Challenges section below).


## ⚙️ How It Works

1. Each bit position has its own **Full Adder**, built from 2 Half Adders + 1 OR gate — all constructed using BC547 transistors instead of ready-made logic ICs.
2. `Cin` of the first stage is grounded (0).
3. Every stage's `Cout` physically ripples into the next stage's `Cin` — so the final bit's sum is only valid once the carry has propagated through all previous stages.
4. Sum bits and the final carry-out are shown live on LEDs — **green LEDs = LSB side**, **red LEDs = MSB side** — so you can visually read the binary result off the breadboard.

